# {{fruits|en}} [[pomme#fr|Pomme]], fruit du pommier (''[[Malus domestica]]'').
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | All '''apples''' eaten ſoone after y&#877; they be gathered, are cold, hard to digeſt, and do make ill and corrupted bloud, but being wel kept vntill y&#868; next winter, or the year folowing, eatẽ [eaten] after meales, they are right holeſome, & doe confyrme the ſtomacke, & make good digeſtion, ſpecially if they be roſted or baked, […]
 |source={{w|Thomas Elyot}}, ''The castel of helth'', f° 21, {{w|Thomas Berthelet|lang=en}}, Londres, 1539
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/b30320264/page/21/mode/1up}}
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | I have so often heard Mr. Woodhouse recommend a baked '''apple'''. I believe it is the only way that Mr. Woodhouse thinks the fruit thoroughly wholesome. We have '''apple''' dumplings, however, very often. Patty makes an excellent '''apple'''-dumpling.
 |source={{w|Jane Austen}}, {{w|Emma (roman)|''Emma''}}, pages 184-185, 1815
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/emmanovelinthree131aust/page/n192/mode/1up}}
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | Close by and under cover, I watched the juicing process. '''Apples''' were washed, then tipped, stalks and all, into the crusher and reduced to pulp.
 |source=John Vallins, « Apples of Concord » dans ''{{w|The Guardian}}'', 28 octobre 2013
 |lien=https://web.archive.org/web/20230923080841/https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2013/oct/28/somerset-apples-of-concord}}
# Tout autre [[fruit]] qui ressemble à une pomme.
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | The '''apples''' wherewith the Indian Canibales inueneme theyr arrowes, growe on certeyne trees couered with many braunches and leaues beinge very greene and growyng thicke. They are laden with abundaunce of theſe euyll frutes, […]
 |source={{w|Gonzalo Fernández de Oviedo}}, ''Decades of the New World'', f° 198, 1555
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/decadesofnewewor00angh/page/n453/mode/1up}}
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | In Perſia there grovvs a deadly tree, vvhoſe '''apples''' are poiſon, and preſent death: therefore there it is uſed for a puniſhment: but being brought over to the Kings into Egypt, they become vvholeſome '''apples''' to eat, and loſe their harmfulneſſe, as Columella vvrites.
 |source={{w|Giambattista della Porta}}, ''Natural Magick […]'', traduit par John Wright, page 21, 1658
 |lien=https://books.google.com/books?id=WddkXA6n51UC&pg=PA21}}
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | One kind of '''apple''' or gall, inhabited only by one grub, is hard and woody on the outside, resembling a little wooden ball, of a yellowish color, but internally it is of a soft, spongy texture.
 |source=Charles Williams, « The Aged Tree », dans ''The Vegetable World'', 1833
 |lien=https://books.google.com/books?id=_6RgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA192}}
# {{par analogie|en}} Qui ressemble à une pomme.
## [[pomme d’Adam|Pomme d’Adam]], abréviation de {{l|Adam’s apple|en}}.
##* {{exemple|lang=en|tête=##*
 | The sweat of fear and exertion was streaming down his face and chest, and his breath came in short, tearing, hard-drawn gasps and gulps, while the '''apple''' in his throat leaped up and down ceaselessly like a ball balanced on a dancing jet of water.
 |source={{w|Hugh Clifford}}, « In Arcadia », dans ''Studies in Brown Humanity: Being Scrawls and Smudges in Sepia, White, and Yellow'', page 99, 1898
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/studiesinbrownhu00clif/page/99/mode/1up}}
##* {{exemple|lang=en|tête=##*
 | If the Hound had not been moving, the knife might have cored the '''apple''' of his throat; instead it only grazed his ribs, and wound up quivering in the wall near the door. He laughed then, a laugh as cold and hollow as if it had come from the bottom of a deep well.
 |source={{w|George R. R. Martin}}, ''{{w|A Storm of Swords}}'', 2000
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/stormofswordsaso0000geor/page/n4/mode/1up}}
## {{archaïsme|en}} [[iris|Iris]], abréviation de {{l|apple of the eye|en}}.
##* {{exemple|lang=en|tête=##*
 | None have their eies all of one colour: for the ball or '''apple''' in the middeſt is ordinarily of another colour than the vvhite about it.
 |source={{w|Pline l'Ancien}}, ''{{w|Histoire naturelle (Pline l'Ancien)|Natural History}}'', traduit par {{w|lang=en|Philemon Holland}}, page 334, 1601
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/plinyhollandhistorie01/page/334/mode/1up}}
##* {{exemple|lang=en|tête=##*
 | The dart did vndergore / His eye-lid, by his eyes deare rootes; and out the '''apple''' fell, / The eye pierc'd through: […]
 |source={{w|Homère}}, ''{{w|Iliade}}'', traduit par {{w|George Chapman}}, page 197-198, [1611?]
 |lien=https://books.google.com/books?id=ashjAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA198}}
# [[pommier|Pommier]] (''[[Malus domestica]]''), ou un autre arbre du genre ''[[Malus]]''.
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | Trees that beare Maſt, and Nuts, are commonly more laſting, than thoſe that beare Fruits; Eſpecially the Moiſter Fruits: As Oakes, Beeches, Cheſ-nuts, VVall-nuts, Almonds, Pine-Trees, &c. laſt longer than '''Apples''', Peares, Plums, &c.
 |source={{w|Fracis Bacon}}, ''Sylua Syluarum: Or A Naturall Historie. In Ten Centuries.'', 1627, page 146
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_sylva-sylvarum-or-a-natu_bacon-francis-viscount_1626/page/146/mode/1up}}
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | If the grafted portion of an '''Apple''' or other tree were examined after one hundred years, the old cut surfaces would still be present, for mature or ripened wood, being dead, never unites.
 |source=John Weathers, ''Commercial gardening'', page 38, 1913
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/commercialgarden0001john/page/38/mode/1up}}
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | Some fruit trees, like plums, do well in damp soil conditions. Other fruit trees, like '''apples''', need well-drained soil.
 |source=Terri Reid, « Starting an Orchard » dans ''The Everything Guide to Living Off the Grid: A Back-to-basics Manual for Independent Living'', page 77, 2012
 |lien=https://books.google.com/books?id=rQrtDQAAQBAJ&pg=PT78}}
# {{injurieux|en}} {{péjoratif|en}} Un [[amérindien]] ou personne à la peau rouge qui agit ou pense comme une personne blanche ([[caucasienne]]).
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | My ancestors five generations removed were "'''apples'''" who were "White" on the inside and "Red" on the outside. […] We need a new breed of "'''apples'''."
 |source=Joel Spring, « From Thomas Jefferson to Henry Ford: The End of the Choctaw Republic » dans ''The Cultural Transformation of A Native American Family and Its Tribe 1763–1995: A Basket of Apples'', 1996
 |lien=https://books.google.com/books?id=APEvisJqs1oC&pg=PT214}}
#* {{exemple|lang=en
 | The presenter, close to tears, told the audience that she's really an apple—white on the inside and red on the outside—Native American.
 |source=Opal J. Moore, « From the Back of the Bus to the Back of the National Priority List […] » dans ''Honey, Hush! An Anthology of African American Women’s Humor'', page 537
 |lien=https://archive.org/details/honeyhush00dary/page/537/mode/1up}}

