#*{{exemple|lang=en|A Scout is never a '''snob'''; he accepts the other man as he finds him, and makes the best of him.|source=''{{ws|Boy Scouts and What They Do/Scout Law|The Scout Law|lang=en}}''}}
#*{{exemple|lang=en|Then there is that singular anomaly, the Inverted '''Snob''', who balances a chip on his shoulder and thinks that everyone of wealth or social prominence is necessarily to be distrusted; that the rich are always pretentious and worldly, while those who have few material possessions are themselves possessed (like Rose Aylmer) of every virtue, every grace.|source=''Atlantic Monthly'', février 1922}}

